uch particles must be calculated using relativistic formulas, not the classical ones.\n\nHowever, relativity does *not* describe the internal structure or probabilistic behavior of particles in the way quantum mechanics does. Instead, it governs how particles move and interact in space and time when their speeds are very high or when gravity is strong.\n\n---\n\n**3. The Concept of Superposition (in a non-quantum context):**\n\n"Superposition" is a term used in various fields, but in physics, it often refers to a situation where two or more states, waves, or forces combine to form a new, resultant state.\n\nFor example:\n- In **waves**, superposition means that when two waves meet, their amplitudes add together. If one wave peaks upward and another peaks downward at the same point, they can cancel each other (destructive interference) or reinforce each other (constructive interference).\n- In **electric or magnetic fields**, the total field at a point is the sum of all individual fields from different sources.\n\nThis principle is fundamental in understanding sound, light, and many other physical phenomena.\n\nIn the context of quantum mechanics (which we are avoiding), superposition means a particle can exist in multiple states at once until measured. But in classical physics, superposition is simply about combining effects—like how two ripples on a pond merge into a new pattern.\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**\n\n- **Relativity** explains how space, time, and gravity behave, especially at high speeds or in strong gravitational fields.\n- It affects how we describe the motion and energy of particles when they move very fast, but it doesn\'t describe their internal quantum behavior.\n- **Superposition** is a general principle where multiple effects or states combine linearly to form a new outcome—common in waves, fields, and forces.\n\nThese ideas are powerful tools in understanding the physical world, even without entering the realm of quantum mechanics.\n    Important Note